\details{
A V8 context cannot be saved or duplicated, but creating a new context and
sourcing code is cheap. You can run as many contexts as you want. R packages
that use V8n can use a separate context for each object or function call.

The name of the global object (i.e. \code{global} in Node and \code{window} in
browsers) can be set with the \code{global} argument. A context always has a
global scope, even when no name is set. It can always be reached as
\code{globalThis}, or by evaluating \code{this} in the global scope, for example:
\code{ct$eval("Object.keys(this)")}.
}
\section{V8 Context Methods}{

\Sexpr[results=rd, stage=build, echo=FALSE]{V8n:::generate_rd()}

The \code{ct$eval} method evaluates a string of JavaScript code in the same way
as \code{eval} in JavaScript. By default \code{eval()} returns the result as a
string; when the \code{serialize} parameter is set to \code{TRUE} it serializes the
result to a JSON string, or to a raw vector for array buffers.

The \code{ct$get}, \code{ct$assign} and \code{ct$call} functions convert arguments and
return values between R and JavaScript. To pass literal JavaScript
arguments that should not be converted, wrap them in \code{JS()}, see examples.

If a call to \code{ct$eval()}, \code{ct$get()}, or \code{ct$call()} returns a JavaScript
promise, you can set \code{await = TRUE} to wait for the promise to be resolved.
It will then return the result of the promise, or an error in case the
promise is rejected.

JavaScript runs until it finishes. Press Ctrl+C or Esc to stop it, or set
\code{timeout} (in seconds) in \code{ct$eval()}, \code{ct$get()} or \code{ct$call()} to stop it
with an error after that time. When JavaScript runs out of memory, the call
fails with an error instead of ending the R session. The heap is sized by
the memory of the machine; \verb{options(V8n.heap_limit = <MB>)}, set before the
package is loaded, sets it instead. With pointer compression, as in the
static builds of libv8, the heap is at most 3000MB.

Some builds of libv8 read their Unicode and locale data (ICU) from a file
\code{icudtl.dat}, which V8n looks for next to the library. If it is missing,
\code{Intl} and the locale functions of JavaScript, such as \code{localeCompare()},
give an error. \verb{options(V8n.icu_data = <path>)}, set before the package is
loaded, names the file. \code{engine_info()} reports the state as \code{icu}:
\code{"none"} for builds without ICU, \code{"available"} or \code{"missing"}.

The \code{ct$validate} function is used to test if a piece of code is valid
JavaScript syntax within the context, and always returns \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}.

\code{ct$source} evaluates a file or URL, and \code{ct$reset} replaces the context by
a new, empty one.
}

\section{Data Interchange}{

Arguments and return values are converted between R and JavaScript
according to the mapping described in
\href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.2805}{Ooms (2014)}, the same mapping that the
jsonlite package implements. Values are copied directly between R vectors
and JavaScript typed arrays, without going through JSON text. Vectors,
lists, matrices and data frames are converted in both directions, data
frames column by column.

The options of \code{toJSON()} from jsonlite can be passed to \code{ct$assign()} and
as an options object to \code{console.r.get()}: \code{dataframe}, \code{matrix}, \code{Date},
\code{POSIXt}, \code{factor}, \code{complex}, \code{raw}, \code{null}, \code{na}, \code{auto_unbox}, \code{digits},
\code{UTC}, \code{rownames}, \code{keep_vec_names}, \code{json_verbatim} and \code{time_format}.
The options of \code{fromJSON()} can be passed to \code{ct$get()} and as an options
object to \code{console.r.assign()}: \code{simplifyVector}, \code{simplifyDataFrame},
\code{simplifyMatrix} and \code{flatten}. \code{ct$call()} passes \code{simplify} on as
\code{simplifyVector}.

Some defaults differ from jsonlite: doubles keep full precision (jsonlite
rounds to 4 decimal digits unless \code{digits} is set), \code{NA} in vectors becomes
\code{null} (jsonlite writes the string \code{"NA"} for numbers), \code{NaN} and \code{Inf}
stay numbers, raw vectors become \code{Uint8Array} also inside lists, and whole
numbers from JavaScript are returned as integers whenever they fit.

Raw vectors are converted to \code{Uint8Array} typed arrays, and array buffers
and typed arrays to raw vectors. This makes it possible to copy large
chunks of binary data between R and JavaScript, which is useful for running
\link{wasm}.
}

\section{Web APIs}{

Besides JavaScript itself, every context provides some of the APIs of
browsers and Node that libraries commonly expect: \code{setTimeout()},
\code{setInterval()} and their \code{clear} functions, \code{queueMicrotask()},
\code{structuredClone()}, \code{TextEncoder} and \code{TextDecoder} (UTF-8 and UTF-16LE),
\code{atob()} and \code{btoa()}, \code{URL} and \code{URLSearchParams},
\code{crypto.getRandomValues()} and \code{crypto.randomUUID()}, and
\code{performance.now()}. Timers run after each call from R and while waiting
for a promise with \code{await = TRUE}. There is no DOM, no network access and
no file access.
}

\section{Callbacks to R}{

JavaScript code can call back into the R session with \code{console.r.call()},
\code{console.r.get()}, \code{console.r.eval()} and \code{console.r.assign()}, see the
vignette.
}

\examples{
# Create a new context
ctx <- v8()

# Evaluate some code
ctx$eval("var foo = 123")
ctx$eval("var bar = 456")
ctx$eval("foo+bar")

# Functions and closures
ctx$eval("JSON.stringify({x:Math.random()})")
ctx$eval("(function(x){return x+1;})(123)")

# Objects
ctx$assign("mydata", mtcars)
ctx$get("mydata")
outlist <- ctx$get("mydata", simplifyVector = FALSE)
outlist[1]

# Assign JavaScript
ctx$assign("foo", JS("function(x){return x*x}"))
ctx$assign("bar", JS("foo(9)"))
ctx$get("bar")

# Validate script without evaluating
ctx$validate("function foo(x){2*x}") #TRUE
ctx$validate("foo = function(x){2*x}") #TRUE
ctx$validate("function(x){2*x}") #FALSE

# Call anonymous function
ctx$call("function(x, y){return x * y}", 123, 3)

\dontrun{
# Use a JavaScript library
ctx$source(paste0("https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/",
                  "underscore.js/1.13.6/underscore-min.js"))
ctx$call("_.filter", mtcars, JS("function(x){return x.mpg < 15}"))

# Example from underscore manual
ctx$eval("_.templateSettings = {interpolate: /\\\\{\\\\{(.+?)\\\\}\\\\}/g}")
ctx$eval("var template = _.template('Hello {{ name }}!')")
ctx$call("template", list(name = "Mustache"))
}
}
